WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:09.350
Imagine a planet of endless sand and scorching
sun.

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:15.820
A world where every day is a race for survival,
and every night a fearsome competition for

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:17.060
resources.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:23.090
The infamous desert planet of Tatooine — located
in a galaxy far, far away — is known for

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:25.550
being an inhospitable wasteland.

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:30.689
Yet the creatures that brave this unforgiving
environment are among the most fascinating,

00:00:30.689 --> 00:00:34.381
and possess hidden intricacies that deserve
to be studied further.

00:00:34.381 --> 00:00:41.129
So, for this entry into the archive, I’ll
be documenting this arid biosphere firsthand

00:00:41.129 --> 00:00:47.170
— analyzing the biology of these extreme
lifeforms, and seeing how their survival strategies

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:49.980
compare to those present in Earth’s deserts.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:55.749
So, join me in a landspeeder on this voyage
across the desert, as we uncover the mysteries

00:00:55.749 --> 00:00:59.390
of Tatooine.

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:05.560
The planet of Tatooine orbits a rare binary
star system, meaning it has two suns — with

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:09.960
the smaller sun in a stable orbit around the
larger one.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:15.659
On the surface of Tatooine, one can certainly
feel the heat of both suns at all times — as

00:01:15.659 --> 00:01:18.159
can the animals that live here.

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:23.229
Beginning our journey on the vast expanse
known as the Dune Sea, life in this region

00:01:23.229 --> 00:01:28.150
is highly specialized to survive the merciless
heat.

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:33.110
Peering through my Macrobinoculars, I spot
a group of wooly Banthas.

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:37.990
These hardy mega-faunae are the preferred
method of transportation for the local Tusken

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:40.780
populace, and a staple of Tatooine wildlife.

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:47.190
A large, furry animal might seem poorly suited
for a hot desert environment, but we can find

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:51.990
a lifeform with a similar strategy in the
Earth camel.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:57.030
Camels are experts at keeping cool, in part
through specialized shaggy fur that helps

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:01.250
keep their body temperatures low and prevents
excess moisture loss.

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:08.050
It’s likely the Banthas can endure the deserts
of Tatooine thanks to similar adaptations.

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:10.009
But beating the heat is one thing.

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:15.640
To survive on Tatooine, the Banthas must also
find food, and water.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:20.090
To discover the Bantha’s food source, one
must dig underground.

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:25.890
This is a black melon, a cactus-like plant
species which, like many forms of cacti, is

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:29.830
a precious source of water in this arid ecosystem.

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:35.030
Once again, Earth camels sustain their bulk
on a similar food source, grazing on cacti

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:37.260
whenever they get the chance.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:42.360
While you or I might struggle to stomach plants
like this, it’s no trouble for desert specialists

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.400
like the camel or the Bantha.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:51.590
So, while the Dune Sea seems barren, the surprisingly
dense network of underground vegetation means

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:58.530
large creatures such as the Bantha don’t
go hungry — if they know where to look.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:01.900
But it’s not just Banthas who drink of the
Black Melon.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:08.010
The Tusken Clans, groups of mysterious sapient
life-forms that call this unforgiving desert

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:11.760
their home, also know all the ins and outs
of the Dune Sea.

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:17.090
I initially hoped to document some of their
knowledge, but Tuskens are highly distrustful

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:18.180
of off-worlders.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:23.940
They weren’t interested in conversing without
their masks—or even with them, so my questions

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:27.540
about this planet’s ecology go unanswered
for now.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:32.590
Feeling my welcome is well past worn out,
it’s time I set off on my landspeeder once

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:37.090
more, and document the next lifeform of Tatooine…

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:40.770
Heading through a narrow canyon, I spot a
fearsome Massiff.

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:46.760
And unfortunately for me, these mid-sized
reptilian predators move in packs, coordinating

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:50.110
in intricate groups to take down their prey.

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:55.290
The Massiff pack structure is similar to those
of spotted hyenas on Earth, with both species

00:03:55.290 --> 00:04:00.819
living in large factions that coordinate to
apprehend animals far larger than themselves

00:04:00.819 --> 00:04:04.950
— and sharing in the spoils once the hunt
is over.

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:10.900
Fortunately for me, this pack, surprisingly,
isn’t wild, but are domesticated allies

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:14.120
of the same Tusken Clan I spoke to before.

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:20.660
As it turns out, domesticated Massiffs serve
as loyal guard dogs across Tusken societies.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:25.500
Deciding to spend another night with the clan,
I learn more about the Massiffs, like how

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:29.870
their dorsal spines aren’t just for defense,
but also show the creatures’ age through

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:30.870
their length.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:34.699
And although I’m still a little worried
the Massiffs haven’t stopped thinking about

00:04:34.699 --> 00:04:40.660
eating me, I’m excited to find the Tuskens
are opening up to my questions.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:45.819
A Tusken storyteller I interview informs me
that their people believe that thousands of

00:04:45.819 --> 00:04:52.380
years ago, Tatooine was covered in rainforest,
and the Dune Sea itself was an ocean of flowing

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:53.500
water.

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:58.330
Such an extreme transformation might seem
unlikely, but shells and other fossils found

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:02.849
in the Dune Sea on a prior expedition corroborate
this theory.

00:05:02.849 --> 00:05:08.999
The process by which fertile land becomes
desert is called Desertification, and it seems

00:05:08.999 --> 00:05:14.120
this process began on Tatooine around 25,000
years ago.

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:19.260
Some claim the desertification of Tatooine
was brought on by off-worlder activity, while

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:23.229
others theorize it was a natural process brought
on by the planet’s twin suns.

00:05:23.229 --> 00:05:28.530
In any case, it’s something I’ll have
to keep in mind as I consider the lineage

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:31.350
of the species that live here.

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:34.780
The next morning, a commotion stirs through
the camp.

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:37.729
A trap has caught a nocturnal Worrt.

00:05:37.729 --> 00:05:41.530
Twisting out, the toad-like creature careens
across the sand.

00:05:41.530 --> 00:05:46.120
Typically, Worrts are most active at night,
when they feed on rodents like womp rats…

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:50.289
and anything else they can ambush with their
shockingly long tongues.

00:05:50.289 --> 00:05:55.419
These tongues resemble those of Earth chameleons,
which can also lash out with amazing speed

00:05:55.419 --> 00:05:57.590
at unsuspecting prey.

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:02.150
Like the Wortt, the Chameleon’s tongue is
stretchable to almost twice the animal's length,

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:08.230
and is powered by spirally wound protein fibers,
which can store energy like a stretched rubber

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:09.230
band.

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:12.099
Yet soon this predator will become prey.

00:06:12.099 --> 00:06:15.699
One quick blaster bolt… and it’s all over.

00:06:15.699 --> 00:06:19.760
This Worrt will help feed the clan and their
Massiffs a little longer.

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:24.509
To survive on Tatooine, one must take what
one can get…

00:06:24.509 --> 00:06:29.760
Setting off towards the western reaches, I
spot, in the distance, an intimidating creature

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:32.199
rising up from the sand.

00:06:32.199 --> 00:06:36.270
This six-limbed feral beast is a Tatooine
Sand Ape.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:41.930
Its target is a wild Massiff, which the giant
Sand Ape makes short work of.

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:44.650
This is not a creature one should mess with.

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:50.080
While their name and muscular anatomy mirror
large primates like gorillas, the Sand Ape’s

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:56.800
ambush hunting strategy is actually much closer
to a very different kind of lifeform.

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:01.919
In the Sahara Desert, a slightly smaller predator
swims under the sand.

00:07:01.919 --> 00:07:06.550
The Sandfish is a type of reptile that buries
itself within the dunes.

00:07:06.550 --> 00:07:12.260
Then, when prey gets close enough it surfaces
and snatches it up — just like the Sand

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:13.260
Ape of Tatooine.

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:18.009
And now that this Sand Ape has subdued the
Massiff, it’s time to head in the other

00:07:18.009 --> 00:07:21.919
direction, before it makes me its next meal…

00:07:21.919 --> 00:07:27.319
Cresting over a sand dune, I spot a herd of
lethargic Dewbacks.

00:07:27.319 --> 00:07:32.599
These thick-skinned reptiles are frequently
used as mounts, both by travelers, and those

00:07:32.599 --> 00:07:34.879
of a more sinister nature.

00:07:34.879 --> 00:07:39.130
Their name, however, comes from their seldom
glimpsed habit of licking the morning dew

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:41.639
from their backs to stay hydrated.

00:07:41.639 --> 00:07:47.550
A desert animal drinking collected dew might
sound miraculous, but Namib Desert Beetles,

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:53.210
which I’ve discussed on this channel before,
actually have a convergent survival strategy.

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:58.280
On misty desert mornings, they stand on their
heads and let the dew that collects on their

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:00.779
backs drip down into their mouths.

00:08:00.779 --> 00:08:06.030
And with fog in the air today, these Dewbacks
likely have the same idea.

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:10.979
On a world with Twin Suns, all water is precious…

00:08:10.979 --> 00:08:15.900
But there are more dangerous creatures under
the Twin Suns I’ve yet to document.

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:21.939
Rancors are massive predators prized in the
underground market for their fighting capabilities.

00:08:21.939 --> 00:08:27.490
Paying to study one specimen, I’ve found
they live up to their fearsome reputation.

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:33.010
With their long arms, they seem somewhat unsteady
on two feet, with a posture reminiscent of

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:36.510
the fighting stance of a territorial grizzly
bear.

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:41.430
With their long claws and powerful brawn,
grizzlies are perhaps the closest things on

00:08:41.430 --> 00:08:43.340
Earth to a Rancor.

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:49.070
On their hind legs, these bears can tower
over seven feet, or two meters tall.

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:55.260
But as imposing as grizzlies are, a full-grown
Rancor can easily reach sixteen feet, or five

00:08:55.260 --> 00:09:00.330
meters tall — which puts into perspective
just how dangerous these gigantic hulks of

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:02.000
muscle really are.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:07.140
And since Rancors are not native to Tatooine,
their introduction has wreaked havoc on the

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:09.770
local ecosystem…

00:09:09.770 --> 00:09:15.520
But much like grizzly bears, there’s another
side to the seemingly vicious Rancor.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:20.830
My studies suggest young Rancors actually
imprint on whatever species they first interact

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:24.750
with during an early period of development.

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:29.530
Imprinting isn’t unheard of in earth’s
animal kingdom, and is present in young birds

00:09:29.530 --> 00:09:30.530
especially.

00:09:30.530 --> 00:09:36.740
But it might be a little surprising to see
such a behavior in a beast as brutal as a

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:37.740
Rancor.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:44.090
Like most creatures, Rancors are much more
complicated than they might first appear.

00:09:44.090 --> 00:09:51.770
There’s one in particular that I’ve been
apprehensive to study up close.

00:09:51.770 --> 00:09:56.210
In the Pit of Carkoon dwells the carnivorous
Sarlacc.

00:09:56.210 --> 00:10:02.580
Appearing as a giant mouth buried in the sand,
local testimony indicates that under the surface,

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:07.470
the Sarlacc’s body is over 300 feet, or
100 meters long.

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:13.770
A diagram from the field guide of the interplanetary
naturalist Terryl Whitlatch gives us a more

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:17.640
complete look at the Sarlacc’s body underneath
the sand.

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:22.960
This diagram shows us that feeding tentacles
extend from the creature’s posterior end,

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:26.960
drawing nutrients from the surrounding sand
like the roots of a plant.

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:31.630
Indeed, the ever-confounding Sarlacc may be
more plant than animal.

00:10:31.630 --> 00:10:36.190
But the Sarlacc still feeds on live prey that
falls into its fanged mouth.

00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:43.240
While an organism relying on prey to fall
into its domain might sound strange, one species

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:45.620
on Earth does something quite similar.

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:51.770
This pit belongs to an Antlion, an insect
predator that digs steep holes in the sand

00:10:51.770 --> 00:10:54.810
that almost look like miniature Sarlacc pits.

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:59.620
The Antlion waits at the bottom of the trap
for an ant to tumble down the slope, which

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:02.170
the Antlion then slowly devours.

00:11:02.170 --> 00:11:09.030
Curiously, there are rumors that the Sarlacc
also digests its food over a long period of

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:10.030
time.

00:11:10.030 --> 00:11:14.660
But the only way to determine whether or not
this is true, is to get a closer look.

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:19.640
So I’m lowering a flashlight-equipped camera
inside the creature’s gullet.

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:25.860
This is a nerve-wracking operation, as the
creature might attack the light at any moment.

00:11:25.860 --> 00:11:30.990
Navigating the camera down a stomach cavity,
I find it’s difficult to see anything.

00:11:30.990 --> 00:11:35.890
Turning on the high-powered beam, we’ve
found the half-digested remains of an unlucky

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:37.200
former meal.

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:42.800
While gross, this discovery seems to confirm
the theory of the Sarlacc’s slow digestive

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:43.800
system.

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:49.110
Unfortunately, it seems the Sarlacc has had
enough of our prodding, and turns aggressive.

00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:54.600
Time to quickly pull the camera back to the
surface, and leave this place forever…

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:58.930
On Tatooine, one might assume the Sarlacc
would be the top predator.

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:04.210
But there is a single, final lifeform on Tatooine
large enough to eat Sarlaccs…

00:12:04.210 --> 00:12:08.430
While refueling at a town, I notice the sand
begins to vibrate.

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:14.170
When the ground shakes like this, every lifeform
— from the tiny scurriers, to the largest

00:12:14.170 --> 00:12:16.890
Banthas — know it means trouble.

00:12:16.890 --> 00:12:19.480
One older Bantha isn’t quick enough.

00:12:19.480 --> 00:12:25.130
From the ground bursts an enormous predator,
which vanishes just as quickly with its meal.

00:12:25.130 --> 00:12:31.170
That was a Krayt Dragon, the most elusive
and deadly creature on Tatooine.

00:12:31.170 --> 00:12:36.210
The bones of juvenile Krayt Dragons can sometimes
be glimpsed upon the dunes.

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:40.940
But when Krayt Dragons reach their adult form,
they’re virtually invincible.

00:12:40.940 --> 00:12:45.220
This will be a very challenging species to
study indeed…

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:49.820
Reuniting with the Tusken Clan from earlier,
I agree to travel with them to the site of

00:12:49.820 --> 00:12:51.300
a Krayt Dragon’s cave.

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:56.420
The Tuskens make a pilgrimage here every month,
as they’ve studied its digestive cycle for

00:12:56.420 --> 00:13:00.900
generations, and feed Banthas to the beast
to keep it satiated.

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:04.400
From the depths of the cave, the creature
emerges.

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:10.070
This time, the creature seems satisfied with
its meal, and retreats back under the sand…

00:13:10.070 --> 00:13:11.820
for now.

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:16.470
According to the Tuskens, the Krayt Dragons
have another terrifying habit when they feel

00:13:16.470 --> 00:13:22.040
threatened: projecting corrosive venom directly
from their mouths, which causes their target

00:13:22.040 --> 00:13:23.630
to dissolve.

00:13:23.630 --> 00:13:28.530
While spitting venom might seem unusual, there
are several species of Spitting Cobras that

00:13:28.530 --> 00:13:30.680
have a similar defense.

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:35.140
When threatened, these snakes can contract
muscles around their venom glands and launch

00:13:35.140 --> 00:13:37.440
venom from their needle-like fangs.

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:42.660
And like the Krayt Dragon, some spitting cobras
can launch their deadly venom more than three

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:45.220
times the length of their bodies.

00:13:45.220 --> 00:13:49.800
Krayt Dragon venom, however, isn’t true
venom, but an acidic fluid that helps them

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:51.410
digest their food.

00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:56.200
We’re fortunate the Krayt Dragon wasn’t
in a spitting mood today…

00:13:56.200 --> 00:14:01.120
Yet one way or another, eventually everything
on Tatooine meets its end.

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:06.470
To the south, an adult Krayt Dragon has died,
and other species benefit from the resources

00:14:06.470 --> 00:14:08.860
its body leaves behind.

00:14:08.860 --> 00:14:13.780
The clan I’m traveling with is particularly
effective at breaking down the different parts

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:17.000
of these leviathans after they expire.

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:20.760
But there is one body part in particular they
seem to be searching for…

00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:25.080
A young hunter has found a rare Krayt Dragon
Pearl.

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:29.120
These pearls are formed the same way as the
pearls of Earth mollusks.

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:35.480
The process begins when a small irritant like
a grain of sand finds its way inside a lifeform.

00:14:35.480 --> 00:14:41.010
A crystalized substance known as nacre then
forms around the irritant, and over time the

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:43.430
layers build up and form a pearl.

00:14:43.430 --> 00:14:49.030
But it’s an uncommon occurance in Krayt
Dragons, and hence finding this valuable pearl

00:14:49.030 --> 00:14:52.540
is a tremendous triumph for the clan…

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:59.430
It's been an incredible trek across Tatooine.

00:14:59.430 --> 00:15:04.980
While all lifeforms here have to endure the
same brutal conditions, no two lifeforms have

00:15:04.980 --> 00:15:08.570
adapted to this planet’s unique challenges
in the same way.

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:13.680
We’ve learned much about these survival
strategies on our expedition, but with the

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:20.050
twin suns high in the afternoon sky, the time
has come at last to say goodbye to this unexpectedly

00:15:20.050 --> 00:15:21.980
teeming planet…

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:24.310
As always, thanks for watching.

00:15:24.310 --> 00:15:29.060
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support to help make these videos possible

00:15:29.060 --> 00:15:35.480
by liking, subscribing, and hitting the notification
icon to stay up to date on all things Curious.

00:15:35.480 --> 00:15:48.190
See you in the next video.

